@@@ problem bump-allocator
@@ meta
{
  "title": "Bump Allocator (Arena)",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "topics": ["Allocators", "Alignment", "Pointer arithmetic"],
  "kind": "design",
  "params": [{"name": "capacity", "type": "int"}, {"name": "operations", "type": "string[]"}, {"name": "arguments", "type": "any[][]"}],
  "examples": [
    {"input": ["64", "[\"alloc\",\"alloc\",\"alloc\",\"alloc\",\"alloc\",\"alloc\",\"used\",\"reset\",\"alloc\",\"used\"]", "[[1,1],[4,4],[8,8],[1,1],[16,16],[32,1],[],[],[64,64],[]]"], "output": "[0,4,8,16,32,null,48,null,0,64]", "explanation": "Each alloc prints the offset of the returned pointer from the buffer start. alloc(4,4) skips bytes 1-3 to reach a multiple of 4. alloc(32,1) needs bytes 48..79 but only 64 exist, so it returns NULL. reset makes the whole buffer available again."}
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Round the current address up to a multiple of align: (addr + align - 1) & ~(align - 1) works because align is a power of two.",
    "Align the address (base + used), not just the offset.",
    "Check that the request fits before committing: offset + size must not exceed capacity. Beware of overflow in that sum."
  ],
  "constraints": ["capacity is a multiple of 64, at most 4096", "align is a power of two, 1 <= align <= 64", "1 <= size <= 10^6"],
  "memory": "The arena never calls `malloc`: it hands out pieces of the buffer it was given. Individual allocations are never freed; `arenaReset` releases all of them at once."
}
@@ description
An **arena** (or *bump allocator*) is the simplest and fastest allocator there is. It owns one big buffer and a counter of how much is used. Allocating means rounding the counter up for alignment, then "bumping" it by the requested size. There is no per-object `free`: everything is released at once with a reset. Compilers, games and servers use arenas for data that lives and dies together (one frame, one request, one compilation unit).

```c
typedef struct Arena {
    unsigned char *base;   // start of the buffer
    size_t capacity;       // its size in bytes
    size_t used;           // offset just past the last allocation
} Arena;

void  arenaInit(Arena *a, void *buffer, size_t capacity);
void *arenaAlloc(Arena *a, size_t size, size_t align);   // NULL if it doesn't fit
void  arenaReset(Arena *a);
```

`arenaAlloc` returns a pointer aligned to `align` bytes (a power of two), or `NULL` if the aligned block would not fit. Afterwards, `used` is the offset just past the new block.

The harness prints each allocation as an **offset** from the start of the buffer, and `used` for the `used` operation. It also writes to every block you return, to check that blocks stay inside the buffer and never overlap.
@@ prelude
#include <stddef.h>
typedef struct Arena {
    unsigned char *base;
    size_t capacity;
    size_t used;
} Arena;
void  arenaInit(Arena *a, void *buffer, size_t capacity);
void *arenaAlloc(Arena *a, size_t size, size_t align);
void  arenaReset(Arena *a);
