@@ editorial
## Approach: align, check, bump

```c
void *arenaAlloc(Arena *a, size_t size, size_t align) {
    uintptr_t start = (uintptr_t)a->base + a->used;                     // first free byte
    uintptr_t aligned = (start + (align - 1)) & ~(uintptr_t)(align - 1); // round up
    size_t offset = (size_t)(aligned - (uintptr_t)a->base);
    if (offset > a->capacity || size > a->capacity - offset)            // no overflow
        return NULL;
    a->used = offset + size;
    return a->base + offset;
}
```

### Why alignment?
A `double` or `int64_t` should start at an address that is a multiple of 8, and SIMD types need 16 or more. Misaligned accesses are slower on x86 and can crash on other CPUs. `malloc` always returns memory aligned for any built-in type, and a custom allocator must offer the same guarantee.

### The rounding trick
For a power of two `align`, `align - 1` is a mask of the low bits (`8 - 1 = 0b111`). Adding it and clearing those bits rounds up to the next multiple. To round an *address*, convert it to `uintptr_t`, an integer type wide enough to hold a pointer.

### The overflow-safe check
`offset + size > capacity` can overflow when `size` is huge (try `size = SIZE_MAX`). `size > capacity - offset` cannot, once you know `offset <= capacity`.

### Why arenas are great
- O(1) allocation: a few arithmetic instructions.
- No per-object bookkeeping, and no fragmentation.
- Leak-proof by design: one reset frees everything.

The trade-off is that you can't free an object on its own. Arenas fit data whose lifetimes end together.

**Complexity:** O(1) per operation.

