  ],
  "hints": [
    "Store the count and the characters together: struct RcStr { int refs; char *text; } or a flexible array member char text[].",
    "rcNew must copy s: the caller's string goes away right after the call.",
    "rcRelease decrements the count and frees everything when it reaches zero, not before."
  ],
  "constraints": ["Operations only use objects that are still alive", "Every test ends with all objects released"],
  "memory": "An object is freed exactly when its count drops to 0. Freeing too early is a use-after-free for the remaining owners; not freeing at 0 is a leak."
}
@@ description
**Reference counting** lets several parts of a program share one object without agreeing on who frees it: every owner calls *retain* when it starts using the object and *release* when it is done. The last release frees it. Objective-C, Swift, Python (`Py_INCREF`), COM and C++'s `shared_ptr` all work this way.

```c
typedef struct RcStr RcStr;     // you define it

RcStr *rcNew(const char *s);        // new object holding a copy of s, count = 1
RcStr *rcRetain(RcStr *r);          // count++, returns r
void   rcRelease(RcStr *r);         // count--, frees everything at 0
const char *rcGet(const RcStr *r);  // the string
int    rcCount(const RcStr *r);     // the current count
```

The operations refer to objects by number: `new` prints the number of the object it created (0, 1, 2, ...), and the other operations take that number.
@@ prelude
typedef struct RcStr RcStr;
RcStr *rcNew(const char *s);
RcStr *rcRetain(RcStr *r);
void   rcRelease(RcStr *r);
const char *rcGet(const RcStr *r);
int    rcCount(const RcStr *r);
