@@ editorial
## Approach: the count travels with the data

```c
struct RcStr {
    int refs;
    char text[];              // flexible array member
};

RcStr *rcNew(const char *s) {
    size_t n = strlen(s) + 1;
    RcStr *r = malloc(sizeof *r + n);   // header and characters in ONE block
    if (r == NULL) return NULL;
    r->refs = 1;                        // the creator is the first owner
    memcpy(r->text, s, n);
    return r;
}

RcStr *rcRetain(RcStr *r) { r->refs++; return r; }

void rcRelease(RcStr *r) {
    if (r != NULL && --r->refs == 0)    // last owner gone
        free(r);
}
```

### Flexible array members
`char text[];` as the last member declares an array whose size is chosen at `malloc` time. `sizeof(struct RcStr)` doesn't include it, so you allocate `sizeof *r + n`. One allocation instead of two means faster creation, one `free`, and better cache locality.

### The rules owners must follow
- Call `retain` **before** storing a pointer somewhere new, and `release` when dropping it.
- Never use an object after your own `release`: even if the count was 2, the other owner may release it next.
- Cycles (A holds B, B holds A) never reach zero: reference counting leaks them, which is why Swift has *weak* references.

**Complexity:** O(1) per operation (plus O(length) for `rcNew`).

