@@ editorial
## Approach: an intrusive free list

```c
struct Pool {
    unsigned char *memory;   // all the blocks, contiguous
    void *freeList;          // first free block
    int available;
};

Pool *poolCreate(size_t blockSize, int blockCount) {
    Pool *p = malloc(sizeof *p);
    if (p == NULL) return NULL;
    p->memory = malloc(blockSize * (size_t)blockCount);
    if (p->memory == NULL) { free(p); return NULL; }
    p->freeList = NULL;
    for (int i = blockCount - 1; i >= 0; i--) {          // chain all blocks
        void **block = (void **)(p->memory + (size_t)i * blockSize);
        *block = p->freeList;                           // store "next" inside the block
        p->freeList = block;
    }
    p->available = blockCount;
    return p;
}

void *poolAlloc(Pool *p) {
    void **block = p->freeList;
    if (block == NULL) return NULL;
    p->freeList = *block;           // pop
    p->available--;
    return block;
}

void poolFree(Pool *p, void *block) {
    if (block == NULL) return;
    *(void **)block = p->freeList;  // push
    p->freeList = block;
    p->available++;
}
```

### Why it works
A free block's bytes aren't used by anyone, so its first 8 bytes can hold a pointer to the next free block. Once a block is handed out, the user may overwrite those bytes, and that is fine, because it is no longer on the list. This is why the minimum block size is `sizeof(void *)`.

### `void **`
`(void **)block` treats the block's address as "a place that stores a `void *`", so `*block = next` writes a pointer into the block's first bytes.

### What a pool can't detect
If the user keeps using a block after `poolFree`, they overwrite the free list's "next" pointer, and a later `poolAlloc` returns garbage. Debug pools fill freed blocks with a pattern (like LeakCode's 0xDD) to catch this.

**Complexity:** O(1) alloc and free; O(blockCount) to create.

