@@ editorial
## Approach: hash index + doubly linked list

```c
typedef struct Node { int key, value; struct Node *prev, *next; } Node;

struct LRUCache {
    int capacity, size;
    Node head;                  // sentinel: head.next = most recent, head.prev = least recent
    Node *index[MAX_KEY + 1];   // key -> node
};

void lruPut(LRUCache *c, int key, int value) {
    Node *n = c->index[key];
    if (n != NULL) {                     // update + mark as used
        n->value = value;
        listRemove(n);
        pushFront(c, n);
        return;
    }
    if (c->size == c->capacity) {        // evict the least recently used
        Node *old = c->head.prev;
        listRemove(old);
        c->index[old->key] = NULL;       // forget it in the index too
        free(old);                       // and give its memory back
        c->size--;
    }
    n = malloc(sizeof *n);
    n->key = key;
    n->value = value;
    pushFront(c, n);
    c->index[key] = n;
    c->size++;
}
```

### Why a sentinel?
A circular list with one dummy node (`head`) has no NULL ends: inserting at the front and unlinking anywhere are the same three or four pointer assignments, with no special cases for an empty list or the first/last node. The sentinel lives inside the cache struct, so it is never allocated or freed separately.

### The two places that point to a node
Each node is referenced by the list *and* by `index[key]`. On eviction both must be cleared before the node is freed, or the index keeps a dangling pointer that a later `get` dereferences.

### Index choices
With keys bounded by 10 000, a plain array is a perfect hash table. For arbitrary keys, use a hash map like the one from *Hash Map with Chaining*.

**Complexity:** O(1) per operation; memory O(capacity + key range).

