@@ editorial
## Approach: implicit free list with splitting and coalescing

```c
typedef struct Header { size_t size; size_t free; } Header;   // 16 bytes

void *heapAlloc(size_t size) {
    if (size == 0 || size > heapSize) return NULL;
    size_t need = sizeof(Header) + ((size + 15) & ~(size_t)15); // keep 16-byte alignment
    for (Header *h = (Header *)heapStart; h != NULL; h = nextHeader(h)) {
        if (!h->free || h->size < need) continue;               // first fit
        if (h->size - need >= sizeof(Header) + 16) {            // split
            Header *rest = (Header *)((unsigned char *)h + need);
            rest->size = h->size - need;
            rest->free = 1;
            h->size = need;
        }
        h->free = 0;
        return h + 1;                                           // just past the header
    }
    return NULL;
}

void heapFree(void *ptr) {
    if (ptr == NULL) return;
    ((Header *)ptr - 1)->free = 1;                              // header is right before ptr
    for (Header *h = (Header *)heapStart; h != NULL; h = nextHeader(h)) {
        if (!h->free) continue;
        Header *n;
        while ((n = nextHeader(h)) != NULL && n->free)          // merge free neighbours
            h->size += n->size;
    }
}
```

### Why the header goes *before* the block
`free(ptr)` receives only a pointer, so the allocator must find the block's size from it alone. Storing the header at `ptr - sizeof(Header)` makes that O(1). It is also why freeing a pointer you moved (`p++`) or overflowing *backwards* is so destructive with a real `malloc`: it misreads or corrupts these headers.

### Coalescing
Free blocks of 200 bytes next to each other are useless for a 1000-byte request unless they are merged. This solution re-scans the heap on each free (O(n)), which is simple and good enough here. Real allocators use **boundary tags** (a footer copy of the size at the end of each block, so the previous block is found in O(1)) and **explicit free lists** (free blocks linked together, so allocation doesn't walk used blocks).

### What real allocators add
- Size classes / bins: separate free lists per size range (glibc, jemalloc, mimalloc).
- Thread-local caches to avoid locking.
- Asking the OS for more memory (`VirtualAlloc`, `mmap`, `sbrk`) when the heap is full.
- Debug modes with guard bytes and poisoning, which is what LeakCode's own allocator does to catch your bugs.

**Complexity:** O(number of blocks) per operation in this simple version.
